---
import {
  heroMedia,
  PHONE_MEDIA,
  PHONE_SIZES,
  WIDE_MEDIA,
  WIDE_SIZES,
} from '../lib/hero-media';

const media = await heroMedia();

const alt =
  'Steeja and Arjun sitting together on a ledge in front of a weathered ochre wall painted with a large white bird.';
---

{/* A photograph, and one bird crossing it.
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    The invitation used to be printed over the picture: a double-hairline frame,
    an eyebrow, the names, a rule, the date, the place, a pill and a Scroll
    label — nine elements, and a heavy wash to carry them. All of it has moved
    onto the card below, which overlaps the bottom of the photo as if laid on
    top of it, and the frame motif went with it.

    What is left here is a caption. It names the couple and the day quietly in
    the bottom-left corner, and the rest of the photograph is allowed to be a
    photograph. The scrim is a pool in that one corner now rather than a wash
    over everything.

    The one addition since is a bird. The wall the couple are sitting against is
    painted with a large white one — it is the first thing the alt text reaches
    for — and a moment after the page settles a two-stroke version of it drifts
    across the top of the frame and leaves. Once, and then never again. The long
    comment on `.hero__bird` below is the interesting part of this file.

    Still zero client JavaScript — the bird is CSS along an offset-path, not a
    sprite sheet and not a canvas — and the <h1> is no longer here: it is on the
    card. This is a <p>. */}
<section class="hero">
  {/* Art-directed by hand: `<Picture>` emits no `media`, so it cannot switch
      crops. Same astro:assets pipeline, same formats and widths. Order matters —
      the browser takes the first `<source>` whose media and type both match, so
      the phone crop is offered in all three formats before the wide frame. */}
  <picture class="hero__media">
    <source
      type="image/avif"
      media={PHONE_MEDIA}
      srcset={media.phone.avif.srcset}
      sizes={PHONE_SIZES}
    />
    <source
      type="image/webp"
      media={PHONE_MEDIA}
      srcset={media.phone.webp.srcset}
      sizes={PHONE_SIZES}
    />
    <source
      type="image/jpeg"
      media={PHONE_MEDIA}
      srcset={media.phone.jpeg.srcset}
      sizes={PHONE_SIZES}
    />
    <source
      type="image/avif"
      media={WIDE_MEDIA}
      srcset={media.wide.avif.srcset}
      sizes={WIDE_SIZES}
    />
    <source
      type="image/webp"
      media={WIDE_MEDIA}
      srcset={media.wide.webp.srcset}
      sizes={WIDE_SIZES}
    />
    <img
      class="hero__photo"
      src={media.fallback.src}
      srcset={media.wide.jpeg.srcset}
      sizes={WIDE_SIZES}
      width={media.fallback.width}
      height={media.fallback.height}
      alt={alt}
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />
  </picture>

  <div class="hero__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  {/* Two arcs meeting at a low point, and deliberately nothing else — no body,
      no eye, no tail. A bird at this size is a gesture, and every detail added
      to it makes it read as clip art instead. The wrapper is a zero-sized
      anchor pinned to the top-right corner; the flight, and the reason the path
      is written out three times, are in the style block below. */}
  <div class="hero__bird" aria-hidden="true">
    <svg
      class="hero__bird-svg"
      viewBox="0 0 40 14"
      fill="none"
      focusable="false"
      aria-hidden="true"
    >
      <path class="hero__bird-wing hero__bird-wing--lead" d="M2 9C6 2.6 14 2.4 20 10.5"></path>
      <path class="hero__bird-wing" d="M20 10.5C26 2.4 34 2.6 38 9"></path>
    </svg>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 100vh;
    min-height: 100svh;
    overflow: hidden;
    isolation: isolate;
    /* Behind the photo for the few frames before it decodes — a warm dark, not
       a white flash. */
    background-color: var(--color-ink);
    color: var(--color-shell);
  }

  .hero__media {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: block;
  }

  .hero__photo {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: 50% 38%;
  }

  /* One corner, and only because the monogram is standing in it.
     -----------------------------------------------------------------------
     This has come down twice. It began as a wash over the whole frame, because
     a nine-element invitation was printed across the middle of it. When that
     moved to the card it became a pool in the bottom-left corner, for the two
     caption lines. The caption is gone now too — nothing is printed on the
     photograph at all — so the only thing left to carry is the monogram, which
     is shell-coloured, sits at the top-left inset, and is not ours to move.

     So: an ellipse in that corner and nothing else. No bottom layer, no
     full-width band. Everything below and right of the mark is the photograph
     as shot. */
  .hero__scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 1;
    background-image: radial-gradient(
      58% 42% at 3% 0%,
      rgb(var(--shade-rgb) / 0.62) 0%,
      rgb(var(--shade-rgb) / 0.34) 38%,
      rgb(var(--shade-rgb) / 0.1) 66%,
      rgb(var(--shade-rgb) / 0) 84%
    );
  }

  /* One bird, once.
     -----------------------------------------------------------------------
     A zero-sized box pinned to the top-right corner of the hero, with the
     drawing hanging out of it. Nothing about that is decorative fussiness: a
     `path()` in `offset-path` is measured from the element's own origin, so
     putting the origin in the top-right corner is what makes a negative x mean
     "left, across the picture" and a positive x mean "the other side of the
     right edge, where nobody can see it yet". Given
     `width: 0; height: 0` there is also no box to reason about — the
     coordinates below are simply where the bird is.

     It is inside a box that already clips and already stacks, so it cannot
     escape the photograph or reach anything below it. Absolute, zero-sized and
     pointer-transparent: it reserves no space, takes no click, and contributes
     nothing to CLS. The only two properties that ever change are
     `offset-distance` on the wrapper and a `scaleY` on one path, neither of
     which the layout engine has an opinion about.

     Faint on purpose — shell at 45%, 28px across, 40px on a desktop. It should
     be caught out of the corner of the eye by somebody reading the card below,
     not watched. */
  .hero__bird {
    /* The default state, and the honest one: not rendered at all. The reveal
       lives in the `no-preference` block below, so under `reduce` — or in a
       browser that does not understand the query — there is no start state,
       no animation and no paint to undo. The markup is inert either way; it
       costs the accessibility tree nothing, since it is `aria-hidden` and has
       no text, and it costs the renderer a `display: none` subtree. */
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    right: 0;
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
    /* Above the photograph and above the corner scrim — it is meant to be in
       the sky, not under the wash. Still inside the hero's `isolation`, so it
       can never rise past the card that overlaps this section. */
    z-index: 2;
    pointer-events: none;
    color: var(--color-shell);
  }

  .hero__bird-svg {
    display: block;
    width: 28px;
    height: auto;
    overflow: visible;
  }

  .hero__bird-wing {
    fill: none;
    stroke: currentColor;
    stroke-opacity: 0.45;
    /* Held at 1.25px however the mark is scaled, the same rule the fronds and
       the rings follow — a hairline that thins with the box turns grey. */
    stroke-width: 1.25;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .hero__bird-svg {
      width: 40px;
    }
  }

  @keyframes hero-bird-cross {
    from {
      offset-distance: 0%;
    }
    to {
      offset-distance: 100%;
    }
  }

  /* Not a flap. One wing flexing along its span, by about a sixth — the amount
     of shape change a gliding bird makes when it trims, not the semaphore of a
     frame-by-frame wingbeat.

     Both numbers here were halved from a first pass that beat twice a second
     and compressed by 28%. That is a truthful wingbeat and the wrong thing
     entirely: repetition at that rate is precisely what peripheral vision is
     built to lock on to, and a mark meant to be caught at the edge of the eye
     was instead the fastest thing on a page whose brief is stillness. Slower
     and shallower it reads as a bird holding a line, which is what a bird
     crossing a photograph of somebody else's wedding should be doing. */
  @keyframes hero-bird-beat {
    from,
    to {
      transform: scaleY(1);
    }
    50% {
      transform: scaleY(0.84);
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .hero__bird {
      display: block;
      /* Level, not tangential. `offset-rotate` defaults to `auto`, which turns
         the element to face along the path — on a shape this symmetrical that
         does not read as banking, it reads as a paper aeroplane pitching. */
      offset-rotate: 0deg;
      /* 6s, once, and `both` so the backwards fill holds it off-frame through
         the 1.2s delay and the forwards fill leaves it off-frame afterwards.
         There is no second pass and no return. */
      animation: hero-bird-cross 6s ease-in-out 1.2s 1 both;
    }

    /* Only the leading wing moves, and that asymmetry is intentional: two wings
       beating in lockstep at 28px reads as a mechanism, one flexing against a
       held one reads as a bird correcting itself. `fill-box` resolves the
       origin against the path's own bounding box rather than the SVG's, so
       `100% 100%` is the low inner point where the two strokes meet — the
       spine. Scaled about anywhere else the wing would swing instead of flex.

       Eight beats of 0.75s is 6s, which is the crossing exactly. It is counted
       rather than `infinite` precisely so that it cannot outlive the flight:
       when the bird is gone, nothing here is still running. The page's only
       other repeating animation is the lantern glow in global.css, and that
       one is a 40s drift of a blurred wash that never arrives or leaves. */
    .hero__bird-wing--lead {
      transform-box: fill-box;
      transform-origin: 100% 100%;
      animation: hero-bird-beat 0.75s ease-in-out 1.2s 8 both;
    }

    /* Why this path is written six times.
       ---------------------------------------------------------------------
       `path()` takes absolute user units and cannot take a percentage, so
       alone among every measurement on this site the crossing cannot be
       expressed relative to the viewport. That leaves a choice with a bad
       answer at each end: a path that stops too soon parks the bird in mid-air
       and leaves it hanging there for good — `both` holds the last keyframe —
       and a path made long enough that it can never do that is one the bird
       has left the frame a third of the way through.

       The second failure is the quiet one, and it was measured rather than
       guessed. An earlier draft used three tiers ending at -700, -1160 and
       -2600. On a 1280px laptop — the commonest desktop width there is — the
       bird was off the left edge at 52% of the path, so a crossing specified
       as six seconds was over in three, and the remaining three were spent
       flying through nothing. The brief asked for a bird that is noticed
       peripherally; what it got was one going twice the intended speed.

       So the tiers are narrow instead, each ending just past the left edge of
       the WIDEST viewport that can ask for it. The arithmetic is
       `(W + start) / (end + start)`, the fraction of the path a viewport of
       width W actually sees, and it now lands between 0.63 and 0.97 across the
       whole range instead of 0.48 — which is to say between about four and six
       seconds of visible flight, against six before it leaves. Every path is
       the same curve, scaled: the seven x values sit at the same proportions
       of the span in all six, so the shape of the glide does not change with
       the screen, only its length.

       The remaining limit is honest and unchanged in kind: the last tier ends
       at -2900, so a viewport past about 2840 CSS pixels — a true ultrawide,
       not a scaled 4K, which reports 1920 — would strand it. Fixing that costs
       either another tier nobody will load or a script, and a script is not
       worth it for a bird.

       The y values stay between roughly 30 and 200 in every variant, which
       keeps the whole crossing in the upper third of the frame and well clear
       of the couple. Each path is two cubic segments rather than a line: out,
       a shallow dip, and a rise away — a glide, not a flight path. */
    .hero__bird {
      offset-path: path('M 32 44 C -36 30, -92 88, -169 72 C -264 56, -389 96, -540 62');
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) and (min-width: 480px) {
    .hero__bird {
      offset-path: path('M 36 48 C -59 32, -137 96, -244 78 C -376 60, -550 104, -760 68');
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) and (min-width: 700px) {
    .hero__bird {
      offset-path: path('M 40 52 C -86 34, -190 104, -333 84 C -509 64, -740 112, -1020 72');
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) and (min-width: 960px) {
    .hero__bird {
      offset-path: path('M 48 78 C -117 50, -253 156, -441 126 C -671 90, -974 166, -1340 108');
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) and (min-width: 1280px) {
    .hero__bird {
      offset-path: path('M 56 86 C -172 54, -360 172, -618 138 C -936 98, -1354 182, -1860 118');
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) and (min-width: 1800px) {
    .hero__bird {
      offset-path: path('M 56 92 C -296 58, -585 184, -984 148 C -1475 104, -2120 196, -2900 128');
    }
  }
</style>
